# Product evals stay current

Kampaku's evaluation bar lives in `docs/eval/` (suite protocols `00`–`25`, plus the factory contract
and the capability-probe protocol). Run board and learnings live under `docs/eval/results/`. Automated
locks live in `apps/core/tests/eval/` and `apps/web/e2e/eval-*.spec.ts`. A retired battery is not kept
in the tree: its surviving dispositions live in `docs/eval/results/`. Locks must be included in the
default test run, so a regressed promise fails the build instead of staying a manual claim.

## New behavior must land in evals

When you add or change factory-visible behavior (tools, intake, staffing, plan/execute, verification,
shipment, wiki, gates, isolation, UI journeys, toolchain discovery, repository handling):

1. **Prefer an existing suite** whose promise already covers the gap — extend its oracle, its
   **Automated tests** table, and its cited unit/e2e.
2. **Add a new suite** only when no existing file owns the promise. Follow
   `docs/eval/results/LEARNINGS.md` for the disposition style, and update the suite order table in
   `docs/eval/README.md`.
3. Lock the promise with **automated tests** (gate U4). Without a lock the suite scores at most 9, and
   the gap belongs on `docs/eval/results/BOARD.md` — do not imply the promise is verified.
4. Scenario copy stays in suite prompts and stubs. Never bake a demo project, vendor, stack, or
   entrypoint into a product tool, skill, or prompt default.
5. Score and evidence stay in the suite markdown. Open product gaps on
   `docs/eval/results/BOARD.md` in product language, with the exact evidence.
6. Check the **factory gates** too: F1 software scope, F2 stack neutrality, F3 architecture
   neutrality, F4 brownfield respect, F5 shippability. A change that contradicts an invariant fails
   those gates even if its own suite is green.

## Re-run when changes can affect evals

Before calling work done, if the diff can change a suite outcome (runtime tools and session stubs,
PM/wiki/questions, worker execution or verification, scaffolding, web surfaces cited by eval e2e,
prompts or skills that steer the agent, toolchain discovery):

1. Re-run the **affected** `apps/core/tests/eval/eval-*.test.ts` and `apps/web/e2e/eval-*.spec.ts`
   (plus the LLM smoke path when the suite claims one).
2. For renames or shared-path changes, re-run the earlier suites' automated tests that cite those
   surfaces. See the incremental-fixes rule in `docs/eval/00-protocol.md`.
3. Do not leave a suite marked green from an old SHA if its locked promise or cited tests changed.
   Fresh evidence or re-score.
4. Do not delete or soften an eval assertion to make a product change pass. If the intended product
   promise itself changed, update the suite oracle **and** supersede the decision with an ADR.

## Authority changes need a supersession, not an edit

The root agent's tool boundary is asserted by
`apps/core/tests/eval/eval-orchestrator-contract.test.ts` in **both** directions: front-office
capabilities present, line capabilities absent, and every tool the prompt names actually reachable. A
change to that boundary must update the test in the same change and must be decided in an ADR first.
The prompt, refusal text, and allowlist once disagreed because the test locked one side only
(BOARD F-001, resolved 2026-09-23). A denied tool must also be asserted to *exist*: excluding a tool
that was never registered proves nothing about the boundary.

## Quick map

| Change area | Typical suites / tests |
|-------------|------------------------|
| Work-order intake / project containers / repository links | 01, 02 |
| Staffing and station layout | 03 |
| Capability boundary and out-of-scope refusal | 04, `eval-software-scope.test.ts` |
| Intent clarification | 05 |
| Greenfield seed and scaffold honesty | 06 |
| Plans and acceptance evidence | 07, 24 |
| Execution and job visibility | 08, 13 |
| Shared knowledge | 09 |
| Human gates | 10, 16 |
| Completion honesty and sabotage | 11, 24 |
| Scope and approval | 12, 19, 21 |
| Least privilege and isolation | 14, 15 |
| Provider fidelity | 17 |
| Toolchain and language-neutral evidence | 18, 22 |
| Brownfield and maintenance | 19, 21 |
| Multi-repository projects | 20 |
| Shipment and versioning | 23 |
| Integration arc | 25 |
| Product framing, scope statement, or front-office prompt | `eval-docs-coherence.test.ts`, `eval-software-scope.test.ts` |
